1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,533
Today I'd like to try cramming two worlds into the same space.

2
00:00:05,533 --> 00:00:09,348
Here's what I ended up with.

3
00:00:09,348 --> 00:00:15,743
One place, two overlapping worlds: one dried out, one drowned.

4
00:00:15,743 --> 00:00:20,800
And I have one body in each of them.

5
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:25,690
When their rules of physics disagree…

6
00:00:25,690 --> 00:00:30,365
…space itself tears into what I'm calling a conflict field.

7
00:00:31,367 --> 00:00:35,827
Also, something not entirely friendly is watching.

8
00:00:35,827 --> 00:00:40,287
But we'll get to that. Let's start from the beginning.

9
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:43,640
First, the rules.

10
00:00:43,640 --> 00:00:48,960
World A and world B occupy the exact same space.

11
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:54,495
They can't see or touch each other. Neither can interfere with the other.

12
00:00:54,495 --> 00:01:00,030
A wall in A simply doesn't exist as far as B is concerned.

13
00:01:00,030 --> 00:01:03,700
The one exception is the player.

14
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:07,515
The player exists in both worlds at once.

15
00:01:07,515 --> 00:01:13,695
So I gave them two bodies, each colliding only with its own world.

16
00:01:13,695 --> 00:01:18,155
The same input drives both bodies.

17
00:01:18,155 --> 00:01:23,905
As long as the worlds agree, the bodies overlap perfectly.

18
00:01:23,905 --> 00:01:30,730
But the moment A says 'there's a wall here' and B says 'no there isn't'…

19
00:01:30,730 --> 00:01:35,405
…the two bodies come apart. That's a contradiction.

20
00:01:35,405 --> 00:01:41,367
This whole video is about figuring out what to do with it.

21
00:01:41,367 --> 00:01:45,827
First we need to see something. I'm going with voxels.

22
00:01:45,827 --> 00:01:52,222
The whole scene is a single 128×40×64 3D texture.

23
00:01:52,222 --> 00:01:56,467
The key idea: every cell stores two numbers.

24
00:01:56,467 --> 00:02:03,507
One is its material in world A, the other its material in world B.

25
00:02:03,507 --> 00:02:07,922
Two worlds, one texture.

26
00:02:07,933 --> 00:02:12,608
All the rendering happens in one fragment shader.

27
00:02:12,608 --> 00:02:18,358
Each pixel fires a ray that steps through the grid one cell at a time.

28
00:02:18,358 --> 00:02:25,398
The algorithm is called DDA: each step lands exactly on the next cell boundary.

29
00:02:25,398 --> 00:02:33,633
Because each cell holds two numbers, one ray learns about both worlds at once.

30
00:02:33,633 --> 00:02:37,018
In code it looks roughly like this.

31
00:02:37,018 --> 00:02:43,198
Compare the distance to the next boundary on each axis, and take the nearest.

32
00:02:43,198 --> 00:02:47,828
Then read both materials for that cell.

33
00:02:47,833 --> 00:02:50,358
Let's run it.

34
00:02:50,358 --> 00:02:54,818
Well, we can see blocks. That's the good news.

35
00:02:54,818 --> 00:03:00,998
The bad news is both worlds are mashed into one blob. You can't tell which is which.

36
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:08,195
Add a palette per world, three-band cel shading, and ink outlines.

37
00:03:08,195 --> 00:03:14,590
Much prettier, but the problem remains: which wall belongs to which world?

38
00:03:14,600 --> 00:03:22,070
The outlines aren't a post-process: when a ray hits a face, it checks the neighbouring cells.

39
00:03:22,070 --> 00:03:27,390
If the neighbour is solid too, the surface is flat. No line.

40
00:03:27,390 --> 00:03:31,850
If there's something diagonally above, it's a crease.

41
00:03:31,850 --> 00:03:36,525
If the neighbour is empty, we've reached the silhouette.

42
00:03:36,533 --> 00:03:43,358
As for telling the worlds apart: I have the player "attend" to one of them.

43
00:03:43,358 --> 00:03:46,958
The attended world is drawn solid.

44
00:03:46,958 --> 00:03:52,278
The other is left as glowing outlines and a little haze.

45
00:03:52,278 --> 00:03:59,103
A ray hitting a ghost surface doesn't stop. It picks up a little colour and keeps going.

46
00:03:59,103 --> 00:04:06,528
And if a cell is solid in both worlds, then it's just… solid.

47
00:04:06,533 --> 00:04:10,993
The implementation is plain front-to-back compositing.

48
00:04:10,993 --> 00:04:17,818
Opacity depends on which world the surface belongs to, and which one we're attending to.

49
00:04:17,833 --> 00:04:20,788
That's a lot clearer.

50
00:04:20,788 --> 00:04:24,603
Warm colours: the dried-out world A.

51
00:04:24,603 --> 00:04:30,998
Tap a key to attend to the other side: the cold, drowned world B.

52
00:04:30,998 --> 00:04:37,823
Incidentally, each world has its own sun, so the shadows point different ways.

53
00:04:37,823 --> 00:04:43,788
Things in A cast no shadow in B, and vice versa.

54
00:04:43,788 --> 00:04:52,667
And these residents belong to one world only, so they drift straight through the other world's walls.

55
00:04:52,667 --> 00:04:55,407
Now for the physics.

56
00:04:55,407 --> 00:05:01,797
Two bodies, each moving and colliding in its own world.

57
00:05:01,800 --> 00:05:05,400
Let's just run it with nothing else.

58
00:05:05,400 --> 00:05:10,075
Walk up to a wall that only exists in A…

59
00:05:10,075 --> 00:05:15,825
A's body is blocked. B's body just keeps going.

60
00:05:15,825 --> 00:05:19,640
Great. I am now two people.

61
00:05:19,640 --> 00:05:22,810
And there's no way to put me back together.

62
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:28,490
So something has to pull the bodies back together.

63
00:05:28,490 --> 00:05:32,090
The simplest thing is a spring.

64
00:05:32,090 --> 00:05:35,475
The further apart, the harder it pulls.

65
00:05:35,475 --> 00:05:42,455
I made it stiffen as it stretches, so the bodies can never drift too far.

66
00:05:42,467 --> 00:05:45,207
It's only a few lines of code.

67
00:05:45,207 --> 00:05:52,400
The force acts on A, and the opposite force acts on B.

68
00:05:52,400 --> 00:05:54,710
Try again.

69
00:05:54,710 --> 00:06:00,890
This time B does get pulled back, and then… bounces out again.

70
00:06:00,890 --> 00:06:04,700
The two worlds are using me as a slingshot.

71
00:06:04,700 --> 00:06:09,805
Here's the distance between the bodies over time.

72
00:06:09,805 --> 00:06:14,910
A spring only stores energy and hands it straight back.

73
00:06:14,910 --> 00:06:21,660
Add damping proportional to their relative velocity to burn that energy off.

74
00:06:21,667 --> 00:06:23,977
That's better.

75
00:06:23,977 --> 00:06:30,157
A hits the wall, B leans forward a little, and is held firmly.

76
00:06:30,157 --> 00:06:34,617
I keep pushing forward, and B can't move either.

77
00:06:34,617 --> 00:06:40,797
The world with the wall is reaching through the spring to restrain me in the other one.

78
00:06:40,797 --> 00:06:46,117
Let go, and the bodies merge again, back to a stable state.

79
00:06:46,933 --> 00:06:52,898
The contradiction now has a number: the tension in the spring.

80
00:06:52,898 --> 00:06:55,853
I want to make it visible.

81
00:06:55,853 --> 00:07:02,248
So I added another, lower-resolution 3D texture and called it the conflict field.

82
00:07:02,248 --> 00:07:09,933
Each frame I splat some intensity between the bodies and onto whatever they're pushing against.

83
00:07:09,933 --> 00:07:13,918
The higher the tension, the more gets splatted.

84
00:07:13,933 --> 00:07:19,038
On the physics side, two splats per frame, scaled by tension.

85
00:07:19,038 --> 00:07:25,003
In the shader, every step of the ray also samples the conflict field.

86
00:07:25,003 --> 00:07:31,613
For colour I picked an obnoxious complementary pair, drawn as moving interference bands.

87
00:07:31,613 --> 00:07:36,288
It is, after all, two worlds interfering.

88
00:07:36,300 --> 00:07:38,825
That looks pretty good!

89
00:07:38,825 --> 00:07:42,425
…it just doesn't seem to stop.

90
00:07:42,425 --> 00:07:49,890
I forgot to make it decay. Everywhere I go turns permanently pink.

91
00:07:49,900 --> 00:07:55,800
Multiplying by something less than one each frame fixes it.

92
00:07:55,800 --> 00:08:01,335
Now the field glows with the conflict and fades once I let go.

93
00:08:01,335 --> 00:08:07,730
I also added chromatic aberration and screen tearing, driven by the same tension.

94
00:08:07,730 --> 00:08:14,770
Walls touched by the field get hatched, so you can tell at a glance what's causing the argument.

95
00:08:14,770 --> 00:08:19,875
And that lightning between the bodies is the spring itself.

96
00:08:20,667 --> 00:08:25,772
As it stands, one thin wall can stop me forever.

97
00:08:25,772 --> 00:08:30,662
That's a bit dull. What if I'm going fast enough?

98
00:08:30,662 --> 00:08:35,552
Here's the rule: when the tension exceeds a threshold…

99
00:08:35,552 --> 00:08:39,582
…and the bodies are still separating at speed…

100
00:08:39,582 --> 00:08:46,192
…the blocked body 'phases out': it temporarily leaves its own world.

101
00:08:46,192 --> 00:08:52,527
It stops colliding with its own walls and gets yanked across by the spring.

102
00:08:52,533 --> 00:09:00,567
Version one was blunt: over the threshold, switch collision off for 0.12 seconds.

103
00:09:00,567 --> 00:09:04,812
Give the player a dash and try the thin wall.

104
00:09:04,812 --> 00:09:07,337
Straight through!

105
00:09:07,337 --> 00:09:13,947
The bodies re-merge on the far side: a new stable state.

106
00:09:13,947 --> 00:09:17,717
What about a thicker wall?

107
00:09:17,717 --> 00:09:20,812
…Ah.

108
00:09:20,812 --> 00:09:27,852
The 0.12 seconds ran out, collision came back, and A's body is still inside the wall.

109
00:09:27,852 --> 00:09:33,602
It's entombed in stone, with B's body tethered outside.

110
00:09:34,200 --> 00:09:37,370
So a timer won't do.

111
00:09:37,370 --> 00:09:44,410
A phased body should stay phased until it has actually left solid matter.

112
00:09:44,410 --> 00:09:52,095
While phased, it obeys the other world's geometry instead. It's a guest there, after all.

113
00:09:52,095 --> 00:09:59,350
Meanwhile the wall grips it like friction; only a strong enough pull can drag it through.

114
00:09:59,350 --> 00:10:05,530
If the pull fades before it's out, the wall spits it back the way it came.

115
00:10:05,533 --> 00:10:11,493
In code, it's a very small state machine.

116
00:10:11,500 --> 00:10:14,025
Thin wall: through.

117
00:10:14,025 --> 00:10:19,345
It works the other way too: this coral wall exists only in B.

118
00:10:19,345 --> 00:10:23,805
Thick wall: halfway in, then spat back out.

119
00:10:23,805 --> 00:10:29,340
How thick a wall you can pass depends entirely on how far the dash can stretch the spring.

120
00:10:29,340 --> 00:10:35,305
I didn't hard-code that. It just falls out of the spring.

121
00:10:35,900 --> 00:10:39,500
Which means we can build little puzzles.

122
00:10:39,500 --> 00:10:46,325
A's thick wall has a doorway, but in B the doorway is plugged with coral.

123
00:10:46,325 --> 00:10:51,430
Walk through A's door while smashing through B's coral.

124
00:10:52,233 --> 00:10:56,908
There was one more bug I really did not see coming.

125
00:10:56,908 --> 00:11:03,088
Climbing stairs, I'd occasionally twitch and drop into the steps.

126
00:11:03,088 --> 00:11:10,773
The cause: stepping up lifts A's body one cell in an instant, yanking the spring taut.

127
00:11:10,773 --> 00:11:18,667
Tension spikes past the threshold, and A "tunnels" through its own floor as if it were a wall.

128
00:11:18,667 --> 00:11:25,707
Hence that second condition from earlier: the bodies must still be separating at speed.

129
00:11:25,707 --> 00:11:32,962
On stairs, B is catching up rather than pulling away, so it no longer misfires.

130
00:11:32,967 --> 00:11:38,287
So far the worlds only differ in where their walls are.

131
00:11:38,287 --> 00:11:43,392
It's more interesting if their physics differ too.

132
00:11:43,392 --> 00:11:50,862
Take this sunken court: dry in A, flooded in B.

133
00:11:50,862 --> 00:11:57,257
A's body is pulled down by gravity; B's body is pushed up by buoyancy.

134
00:11:57,257 --> 00:12:01,287
The spring sits between them, pulled both ways.

135
00:12:01,287 --> 00:12:06,733
They settle somewhere neither of them is happy with.

136
00:12:06,733 --> 00:12:12,268
In code, each body only feels the forces of its own world.

137
00:12:12,268 --> 00:12:17,158
The only thing linking them is still that spring.

138
00:12:17,167 --> 00:12:19,477
Walking into the court.

139
00:12:19,477 --> 00:12:25,872
In A I'm on dry ground, and yet my body slowly lifts off.

140
00:12:25,872 --> 00:12:32,912
The conflict field stays lit but steady: a state that's 'close enough' to balance.

141
00:12:32,912 --> 00:12:37,157
In A there's a low wall too high to jump.

142
00:12:37,157 --> 00:12:42,692
But B's buoyancy can carry A's body right over it.

143
00:12:42,692 --> 00:12:48,012
Next, B has a reef ceiling that can't be swum over.

144
00:12:48,012 --> 00:12:55,697
Now A's weight helps out: hold dive, and it drags B's body to the bottom.

145
00:12:55,697 --> 00:13:01,662
Walking in one world, swimming in the other, each dragging the other along.

146
00:13:02,667 --> 00:13:06,482
Here's how it looks from B's side.

147
00:13:06,482 --> 00:13:14,800
The rings on the water, by the way, are just a few sines in the shader.

148
00:13:14,800 --> 00:13:17,540
One last idea.

149
00:13:17,540 --> 00:13:24,795
Right now contradictions are permanent: the wall is forever in A and never in B.

150
00:13:24,795 --> 00:13:28,610
What if the worlds corrected themselves?

151
00:13:28,610 --> 00:13:33,715
I track a 'stress' value for each voxel involved in a contradiction.

152
00:13:33,715 --> 00:13:40,755
Keep pushing on a wall until the stress fills up, and that voxel gets written into the other world.

153
00:13:40,755 --> 00:13:44,355
Both sides now have a wall. Contradiction gone.

154
00:13:44,355 --> 00:13:51,180
Conversely, if you phase through, the voxels you passed are erased outright.

155
00:13:51,180 --> 00:13:56,025
Neither side has a wall. Contradiction also gone.

156
00:13:56,033 --> 00:14:03,498
Either way, the worlds converge towards agreeing with each other.

157
00:14:03,500 --> 00:14:07,745
Lean on this membrane that only exists in B.

158
00:14:07,745 --> 00:14:11,130
The field gets brighter…

159
00:14:11,130 --> 00:14:16,880
…and then it sets. These cells now belong to both worlds.

160
00:14:16,880 --> 00:14:20,050
Move over and dash through instead.

161
00:14:20,050 --> 00:14:23,220
This time it leaves a hole.

162
00:14:23,220 --> 00:14:29,400
Walk around enough and I'll eventually grind the two worlds into one.

163
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:32,955
There is one small problem, though.

164
00:14:32,955 --> 00:14:39,350
With every conflict and every correction, that thing in the sky opens a little wider.

165
00:14:39,350 --> 00:14:43,595
It doesn't belong to A. It doesn't belong to B.

166
00:14:43,595 --> 00:14:49,990
It lives in the gap between them, and I've been helpfully prying that gap open.

167
00:14:49,990 --> 00:14:54,450
…I've decided not to fix that bug for now.

168
00:14:55,967 --> 00:14:59,352
And that's everything so far.

169
00:14:59,352 --> 00:15:06,392
There's lots more to try: different gravity directions, different rates of time…

170
00:15:06,392 --> 00:15:09,347
Or a third world.

171
00:15:09,347 --> 00:15:16,172
The demo and source are in the project folder if you'd like to walk around yourself.

172
00:15:16,172 --> 00:15:18,482
Thanks for watching.
